                                        <div class="tab-pane fade" id="pills-4" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="History"  ><section class="socis-history" style="text-align: justify; line-height: 1.7; font-size: 15px;">

    <h2 style="text-align: left; font-size: 20px; margin-bottom: 15px;">
        <strong>The School of Computer and Information Sciences (SOCIS)</strong>
    </h2>

    <p>
        The School of Computer and Information Sciences (SOCIS) at
        Indira Gandhi National Open University (IGNOU) has been a pioneer in
        delivering education in Computer Science and Information Technology through
        the Open and Distance Learning (ODL) and online modes. Since its establishment,
        the School has continuously evolved its academic programmes, learning resources,
        delivery mechanisms and research activities in response to technological
        advancements and the changing needs of learners and society. Its journey reflects
        IGNOU's commitment to making quality higher education accessible to learners
        irrespective of geographical, social or economic barriers.
    </p>

    <h2 style="text-align: left; font-size: 19px; margin-top: 25px; margin-bottom: 12px;">
        <strong>Historical Development of ODL and Online Programmes by SOCIS</strong>
    </h2>

    <h3 style="text-align: left; font-size: 17px; margin-top: 20px; margin-bottom: 8px;">
        <strong>1. Establishment and Early Adoption of ODL</strong>
    </h3>

    <ul style="text-align: justify;">
        <li>
            Recognising the growing demand for trained computer professionals and IT
            skills, SOCIS introduced its flagship Bachelor of Computer Applications
            (BCA) and Master of Computer Applications (MCA) programmes in the early
            1990s. These programmes were designed to leverage the flexibility and
            reach of the ODL system.
        </li>

        <li>
            The programmes adopted a learner-centred approach supported by printed
            self-learning materials, audio-visual resources, assignments, counselling
            sessions and practical training, catering to the diverse requirements of
            distance learners.
        </li>

        <li>
            The ODL framework enabled learners from different parts of the country,
            including those facing geographical and professional constraints, to
            pursue formal education in computer applications.
        </li>
    </ul>

    <h3 style="text-align: left; font-size: 17px; margin-top: 20px; margin-bottom: 8px;">
        <strong>2. Expansion and Diversification of Programmes</strong>
    </h3>

    <ul style="text-align: justify;">
        <li>
            With the rapid growth of the IT sector, SOCIS continuously updated and
            diversified its academic offerings through Postgraduate Diploma and
            Certificate programmes in computer applications, information technology
            and related areas.
        </li>

        <li>
            The School incorporated emerging educational technologies, including
            television-based educational programmes through Gyan Darshan and
            radio-based programmes through Gyan Vani, to enhance access to learning
            resources.
        </li>

        <li>
            The development of Regional Centres and Learner Support/Study Centres
            provided learners with opportunities for academic counselling, practical
            training and technical support closer to their locations.
        </li>

        <li>
            This integrated approach helped bridge the gap between theoretical
            learning through self-learning materials and practical exposure through
            learner support facilities.
        </li>
    </ul>

    <h3 style="text-align: left; font-size: 17px; margin-top: 20px; margin-bottom: 8px;">
        <strong>3. Development and Expansion of Online Programmes</strong>
    </h3>

    <ul style="text-align: justify;">
        <li>
            In response to technological advancements and the growing demand for
            flexible digital education, SOCIS progressively expanded from its
            traditional ODL framework towards technology-enabled and fully online
            modes of programme delivery.
        </li>

        <li>
            The School leveraged IGNOU's digital infrastructure and Learning
            Management Systems (LMS) to provide structured online learning, access
            to digital resources and learner support.
        </li>

        <li>
            Programmes such as BCA and MCA, along with other computing and information
            technology programmes, have been offered through online delivery,
            enabling learners to pursue their studies with greater flexibility.
        </li>

        <li>
            The School has incorporated contemporary technologies and digital learning
            resources to enhance learner engagement and support independent and
            collaborative learning.
        </li>

        <li>
            SOCIS has also strengthened opportunities for learners to access
            SWAYAM-based courses and credit-transfer facilities, in accordance with
            the University's applicable academic and credit-transfer framework.
        </li>

        <li>
            These developments have expanded the scope of technology-enabled learning
            and provided learners with additional pathways to acquire academic
            credits and contemporary knowledge and skills.
        </li>
    </ul>

    <h3 style="text-align: left; font-size: 17px; margin-top: 20px; margin-bottom: 8px;">
        <strong>4. Strengthening Research through the Visvesvaraya PhD Scheme – 2025 onwards</strong>
    </h3>

    <p>
        From 2025 onwards, SOCIS has further strengthened its doctoral research
        ecosystem through participation in the Visvesvaraya PhD Scheme for
        Electronics and IT, implemented by the Ministry of Electronics and
        Information Technology (MeitY), Government of India.
    </p>

    <ul style="text-align: justify;">
        <li>
            Under this initiative, SOCIS has received support for three Full-Time
            (FT) PhD candidates and one Part-Time (PT) PhD candidate during the
            academic year 2025–26, in addition to the PhD scholars admitted through
            the University's regular PhD admission process. This has further
            strengthened the School's doctoral research capacity.
        </li>

        <li>
            The scheme aims to enhance doctoral research capacity in Electronics
            System Design and Manufacturing (ESDM) and IT/IT Enabled Services
            (IT/ITES). Its Phase-II framework provides support for Full-Time and
            Part-Time PhD candidates, Young Faculty Research Fellowships and
            Post-Doctoral Fellowships.
        </li>

        <li>
            The scheme also provides provisions for research contingency support,
            international conference participation and visits to laboratories
            abroad, thereby facilitating advanced research and capacity building.
        </li>

        <li>
            Through participation in this national initiative, alongside its regular
            PhD admissions, SOCIS is strengthening opportunities for advanced research,
            doctoral capacity building and innovation in computer and information
            sciences and emerging technology areas.
        </li>
    </ul>

    <h3 style="text-align: left; font-size: 17px; margin-top: 20px; margin-bottom: 8px;">
        <strong>5. Contemporary Expansion into Emerging Technologies</strong>
    </h3>

    <ul style="text-align: justify;">
        <li>
            Building upon its longstanding expertise in computer applications and
            information technology, SOCIS continues to expand its academic portfolio
            into emerging and interdisciplinary areas of computing.
        </li>

        <li>
            The School has strengthened its focus on contemporary domains such as
            Data Science, Artificial Intelligence, Machine Learning, Big Data
            Analytics and other emerging technologies.
        </li>

        <li>
            The introduction of specialised programmes such as the M.Sc. in Data
            Science and Analytics (MSCDSA) and M.Sc. in Artificial Intelligence &
            Machine Learning (MSCAIML) reflects the School's continuing transition
            towards advanced, data-driven and research-oriented areas of computer
            and information sciences.
        </li>

        <li>
            The School continues to update its curriculum and learning practices to
            ensure that learners are equipped with relevant knowledge and skills
            aligned with the evolving requirements of academia, industry and society.
        </li>
    </ul>

    <h3 style="text-align: left; font-size: 17px; margin-top: 20px; margin-bottom: 8px;">
        <strong>6. Impact and Continuing Commitment</strong>
    </h3>

    <ul style="text-align: justify;">
        <li>
            Over the years, SOCIS has provided computer science and information
            technology education to learners from diverse geographical, professional
            and social backgrounds through its ODL and online programmes.
        </li>

        <li>
            Its programmes have contributed to the development of a skilled workforce
            in the fields of computer applications, information technology and
            emerging digital technologies.
        </li>

        <li>
            The School continues to integrate technology, flexibility, learner support,
            online learning, credit mobility and research into its academic ecosystem.
        </li>

        <li>
            Through this continuous evolution, SOCIS remains committed to IGNOU's
            vision of democratising higher education through accessible, flexible
            and technology-enabled learning.
        </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
        Through its journey, SOCIS has evolved from a pioneer in ODL-based computer
        education into a School offering ODL, online education, credit-transfer
        opportunities, doctoral research and programmes in emerging areas such as
        Data Science, Artificial Intelligence and Machine Learning. Its history
        reflects a sustained commitment to combining education, technology,
        accessibility and innovation to prepare learners for the rapidly changing
        digital world.
    </p>

</section></div>
